+# Decision: Change delivery transport
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event: each committed edit is assigned a single event revision, and browsers display a revisioned view. Committed changes must reach up to 800 simultaneous browsers within a 15-second freshness goal, surviving opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second, and a browser disconnected for up to 20 minutes must recover the latest board.
+
+The transport chosen here determines what three downstream nodes must each provide. The **change store** either serves browser cursors or keeps replayable revisions and publishes each committed change reliably. **Delivery** answers polls, manages socket sessions, or publishes through a provider. **Browser sync** polls, reconnects and catches up, or subscribes through a provider's model.
+
+This decision builds on **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event**: whichever transport is chosen, one committed edit still carries exactly one event revision regardless of delivery route. It depends on that decision for the invariant the transport carries, not on its command boundary.
+
+The stakes: a transport that cannot absorb bursts or long disconnects violates the freshness and recovery goals; a transport that couples the board to a vendor adds a recurring fee and a migration cost; a transport that centralizes connection handling adds operational burden the team must carry. No document declares recurring cost or minimum operational burden the overriding criterion.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+The mandatory freshness goal (15 seconds) is background, not an element: five-second polling, self-hosted sockets, and a hosted service all meet it, so it does not by itself drive selection. No candidate fails a gate; the elements below are weighted criteria.
+
+- **Vendor coupling — major.** How strongly the delivery path binds the board to a third-party realtime provider, including the cost of moving off it later.
+- **Connection operations burden — major.** How much connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect machinery the team itself must build and operate.
+- **Recurring operating cost — moderate.** Steady-state cost to deliver changes at board scale, independent of build effort. Turns on the tenet *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted*: both paths are allowed, so the choice is economic and operational, not a permission question.
+- **Recovery after long disconnects — moderate.** How readily a browser disconnected for up to 20 minutes recovers the latest board without an interval of stale state being mistaken for an unchanged board. Turns on the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*, and the recovery goal.
+- **Burst durability — moderate.** How well delivery absorbs 20 edits per second across 800 browsers around opening time. Turns on the tenet *A committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of its delivery route*.
+- **Latency headroom — minor.** Delivered latency below the 15-second goal. Subsecond updates are useful but not required.
+
+Latent criteria considered and weighed: authorization of subscriptions (*browser state is not evidence of permission*) applies as a shared obligation to whichever transport is chosen, so it does not distinguish the options; team familiarity and provider SLA quality were considered but neither was strong enough to move the decision absent a declared preference.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers issue a request every five seconds and read changes committed since their last cursor. Delivery stays stateless request/response; the change store serves cursor queries; no persistent connection exists to manage. It is the least machinery to build and operate.
+
+**Analysis**
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — no third party in the delivery path.
+- Connection operations burden: **strong** — no sessions or reconnect logic; a failed poll is just a failed request.
+- Recurring operating cost: **partial** — reads scale with browser count times poll frequency, so burst periods multiply read load even when little changes.
+- Recovery after long disconnects: **strong** — the next poll returns everything since the browser's cursor.
+- Burst durability: **partial** — many browsers polling during an edit burst amplify reads against the store.
+- Latency headroom: **partial** — up to a five-second lag, within the goal but with the least margin.
+
+**Summary:** minimal operations, at the cost of repeated reads and seconds of lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The service hosts persistent socket connections itself and pushes each committed change to connected browsers as it lands. Browsers reconnect when a socket drops and catch up to the latest revision. The change store keeps replayable revisions and publishes each committed change reliably; delivery owns session and connection capacity.
+
+**Analysis**
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — no third party in the delivery path.
+- Connection operations burden: **weak** — the team owns connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect handling for up to 800 browsers.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — push delivery avoids per-message vendor fees and repeated reads; cost is hosting capacity.
+- Recovery after long disconnects: **partial** — recovery is achievable, but requires replayable revisions plus browser reconnect and catch-up logic built and tested.
+- Burst durability: **strong** — a single push fans out to all connected browsers.
+- Latency headroom: **strong** — subsecond delivery.
+
+**Summary:** fast push without a vendor relationship, at the cost of owning connection and reconnect operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A third-party realtime provider manages connections and fan-out. Delivery wraps the provider; the change store reconciles publication to it; browsers use the provider's subscription model and reconnect behavior.
+
+**Analysis**
+- Vendor coupling: **weak** — the board depends on the provider, and moving off it means reworking delivery and browser sync.
+- Connection operations burden: **strong** — the provider operates connections and reconnects.
+- Recurring operating cost: **partial** — recurring provider fees scale with usage.
+- Recovery after long disconnects: **strong** — the provider handles reconnect and replay within its retention.
+- Burst durability: **strong** — the provider fans out.
+- Latency headroom: **strong** — subsecond delivery.
+
+**Summary:** lowest connection operations and fastest to adopt, at the cost of recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets** is chosen. It is **strong** on vendor coupling (major) and on recurring cost, burst durability, and latency, and it is the only option that is independent of a vendor while absorbing bursts through push rather than repeated reads. It is **weak** on connection operations burden (major) and **partial** on recovery — the two costs the team accepts explicitly. Five-second polling is strongest on operations burden but **partial** on latency and burst durability; the hosted service is strong on operations but **weak** on the major vendor-coupling criterion. Neither trade was accepted.
+
+The decision turns on *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* — with both paths open, the board takes the one that keeps delivery independent of a vendor — while preserving *A committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of its delivery route*, which holds because the transport carries only committed revisions. It acts against no tenet.
+
+Committing obligates downstream work: the **change store** must keep replayable revisions and publish each committed change reliably for socket fan-out; **delivery** must own WebSocket session and connection capacity; **browser sync** must reconnect and catch up to the latest revision after a drop. It forecloses, for the pilot, a vendor-managed delivery path and the minimal-operations posture of polling; revisiting it means reworking all three downstream nodes.
